/**
\defgroup quadrature Quadrature rules
\brief Quadrature rules callable inside Kokkos kernels on the host and device: double-exponential, adaptive Gauss-Kronrod, geometric panels, and fixed Gauss rules.

This module provides one-dimensional quadrature rules in `<laplace/math/quadrature.hpp>` (`laplace::math::quad`), marked `LAPLACE_HD` to run on host and Kokkos device without dynamic allocation, recursion, or unbounded loops. On reference integrals, double-exponential and panel rules reach 2.8 to 68 ulp on gcc (2.8 to 21 ulp on CUDA), and Gauss-Kronrod reaches 72 ulp.

## Definitions

This module approximates
\f[
I = \int_a^b f(x) \, \mathrm{d}x,
\f]
where \f$a < b\f$ and \f$f\f$ is a real integrand functor. For \ref laplace::math::quad::tanh_sinh_quad "tanh_sinh_quad()" and \ref laplace::math::quad::exp_sinh_quad "exp_sinh_quad()", \f$f\f$ may alternatively take \f$(x, x_c)\f$, where \f$x_c\f$ is signed distance to the nearer endpoint.

Adaptive rules return \ref laplace::math::quad::quad_result "quad_result" (`value`, `error`, `evals`, `converged`). Double-exponential and panel rules accept \ref laplace::math::quad::de_quad_options "de_quad_options" (`stop`, default \f$10^{-9}\f$; `max_level`, default 8). Fixed Gauss rules return `double`.

The table below summarizes all rules.

| Rule | Domain | Integrand | Return | Intended use |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| \ref laplace::math::quad::tanh_sinh_quad "tanh_sinh_quad()" | Finite \f$[a, b]\f$ | `f(x)`, `f(x, x_c)` | `quad_result` | Smooth or endpoint-singular |
| \ref laplace::math::quad::exp_sinh_quad "exp_sinh_quad()" | \f$[a, \infty)\f$ | `f(x)`, `f(x, x_c)` | `quad_result` | Decaying or endpoint-singular tails |
| \ref laplace::math::quad::sinh_sinh_quad "sinh_sinh_quad()" | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | `f(x)` | `quad_result` | Decaying integrands near \f$c\f$ |
| \ref laplace::math::quad::gauss_kronrod_quad "gauss_kronrod_quad()" | Finite \f$[a, b]\f$ | `f(x)` | `quad_result` | Smooth or interior-kinked |
| \ref laplace::math::quad::panels_quad "panels_quad()" | General \f$[a, b]\f$ | `f(x)` | `quad_result` | Kinks or mass far from \f$c\f$ |
| \ref laplace::math::quad::gauss_legendre_quad "gauss_legendre_quad\<N\>()" | Finite \f$[a, b]\f$ | `f(x)` | `double` | Smooth; exact to degree \f$2N - 1\f$ (\f$N \in \{16, 32, 64\}\f$) |
| \ref laplace::math::quad::gauss_hermite32_quad "gauss_hermite32_quad()" | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | `f(x)` | `double` | 32-node rule, weight \f$e^{-y^2}\f$ |
| \ref laplace::math::quad::gauss_laguerre32_quad "gauss_laguerre32_quad()" | \f$[a, \infty)\f$ | `f(x)` | `double` | 32-node rule, weight \f$e^{-y}\f$ |

## Domain and special values

Finite-interval rules (`tanh_sinh_quad()`, `gauss_kronrod_quad()`, `gauss_legendre_quad()`) require finite \f$a, b \in \mathbb{R}\f$; semi-infinite (`exp_sinh_quad()`, `gauss_laguerre32_quad()`) and infinite-line (`sinh_sinh_quad()`, `gauss_hermite32_quad()`) rules require finite \f$a\f$ or \f$c\f$ and scale \f$s > 0\f$. `panels_quad()` treats \f$a \le -10^{308}\f$ as \f$-\infty\f$ and \f$b \ge 10^{308}\f$ as \f$+\infty\f$.

For binary \f$f(x, x_c)\f$, `tanh_sinh_quad()` passes \f$x_c = a - x\f$ (left half) and \f$x_c = b - x\f$ (right half), while `exp_sinh_quad()` passes \f$x_c = x - a\f$; nodes rounding to an endpoint with \f$x_c \ne 0\f$ are still evaluated. Double-exponential rules skip non-finite values or \f$|v| \ge 10^{308}\f$ (via \f$|v| < 10^{308}\f$), and `sinh_sinh_quad()` skips \f$|\sinh(q)| \ge 10^{300}\f$.

When \f$b < a\f$, explicit-endpoint rules (`tanh_sinh_quad()`, `gauss_kronrod_quad()`, `gauss_legendre_quad()`, `panels_quad()`) negate \f$\int_a^b f = -\int_b^a f\f$, preserving error, evaluations, and convergence. When \f$a = b\f$, those adaptive rules return value 0, error 0, `converged = true`, and zero evaluations, while `gauss_legendre_quad()` returns \f$0.0\f$.

Invalid inputs (non-finite endpoint on finite-interval rules, NaN boundary in `panels_quad()`, non-finite start or centre, or \f$s\f$ not positive and finite) return NaN value and error, `converged = false`, and zero evaluations for adaptive rules, or quiet NaN for fixed semi-infinite and infinite rules.

## Method

### Double-exponential quadrature

Double-exponential rules (Takahasi and Mori, 1974) map \f$x\f$ to \f$t\f$ with \f$q = \frac{\pi}{2}\sinh t\f$ and \f$w(t) = \frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}t}\f$:

1. Finite \f$[a, b]\f$ (`tanh_sinh_quad()`): \f$x(t) = \frac{a+b}{2} + \frac{b-a}{2}\tanh q\f$, endpoint distance \f$d = (b - a)\frac{e^{-2|q|}}{1 + e^{-2|q|}}\f$, and \f$w(t) = \frac{\pi}{2}\cosh(t)(b - a)\frac{2e^{-2|q|}}{(1 + e^{-2|q|})^2}\f$.
2. Semi-infinite \f$[a, \infty)\f$ (`exp_sinh_quad()`): \f$x(t) = a + s e^q\f$, \f$w(t) = s\frac{\pi}{2}\cosh(t)e^q\f$.
3. Infinite \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ (`sinh_sinh_quad()`): \f$x(t) = c + s \sinh q\f$, \f$w(t) = s\frac{\pi}{2}\cosh(t)\cosh q\f$.

After tail trimming, step \f$h\f$ halves up to `max_level` (default 8), summing \f$S = \sum v_k\f$ and \f$L_1 = \sum |v_k|\f$ with \f$v_k = f(x_k)w(k h)\f$. Refinement stops when \f$|\mathrm{est} - \mathrm{prev}| \le \mathrm{stop} \cdot L_1 h\f$ (default \f$10^{-9}\f$), yielding error \f$\mathrm{rel}^2 L_1 h\f$ for \f$\mathrm{rel} = |\mathrm{est} - \mathrm{prev}|/(L_1 h)\f$; otherwise `value` is `est` and `error` is \f$|\mathrm{est} - \mathrm{prev}|\f$.

### Adaptive Gauss-Kronrod quadrature

`gauss_kronrod_quad()` runs a Boost.Math 7–15 Gauss-Kronrod rule on \f$[a, b]\f$, bisecting the subinterval with largest error until \f$\sum \mathrm{err}_i \le \mathrm{tol} \cdot |\sum \mathrm{val}_i|\f$ (default \f$10^{-14}\f$), `max_intervals` (cap 64) are reached, or bisection underflows; it fails on endpoint singularities.

### Geometric panel quadrature

`panels_quad()` cuts \f$[a, b]\f$ at \f$c\f$ and up to 8 sorted interior `kinks`, subdivides finite subpieces from the end nearer \f$c\f$ into up to 64 doubling panels \f$s, 2s, 4s, \dots\f$ via `tanh_sinh_quad()`, and integrates tails beyond \f$63s\f$ via `exp_sinh_quad()` at scale \f$s\f$; `converged` requires all panels to converge.

### Fixed Gauss rules

Nodes and weights come from 256-bit Golub-Welsch. `gauss_legendre_quad<N>()` (\f$N \in \{16, 32, 64\}\f$) is exact to degree \f$2N - 1\f$. `gauss_hermite32_quad()` and `gauss_laguerre32_quad()` evaluate \f$s\sum_{i=0}^{31} w_i e^{y_i^2} f(c + s y_i)\f$ and \f$s\sum_{i=0}^{31} w_i e^{y_i} f(a + s y_i)\f$.

## Accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for metrics and precision. Tests cover 540 reference integrals (9 integrands at 60 parameter values, from closed forms or 200-bit `mpmath`); \f$p\f$ is the parameter and \f$\phi, \Phi\f$ are the standard normal density and CDF. Cells give maximum relative error in ulp on gcc (CUDA in parentheses where different), mean evaluations, and `[bound]` in ulp; every reported row converged.

| Integrand | tanh-sinh | exp-sinh | sinh-sinh | panels | GK15 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Beta (singular ends, with distance) | 6.5 (7.8), 88 [10.0] | — | — | — | fails |
| Gamma (singular end, \f$a \le 100\f$) | — | 68 (20), 136 [100.0] | — | 13 (21), 839 [32.0] | — |
| Student t tail (\f$\nu \ge 0.25\f$) | — | 5.4, 85 [8.0] | — | 4.0, 793 [6.0] | — |
| Truncated Laplace (kink at 0) | — | — | — | 4.5, 229 [7.0] | 72, 167 [110.0] |
| \f$x^p \log(x)^2\f$ (\f$p + 1 \ge 0.1\f$) | 6.4 (7.4), 72 [10.0] | — | — | 3.9, 274 [6.0] | fails |
| \f$2 x^2 \phi(x) \phi(p x)^2 / \Phi(p x)\f$ | — | — | 7.3 (6.7), 159 [11.0] | 6.8 (5.4), 1527 [10.0] | — |
| \f$\exp(-p(\sqrt{1 + x^2} - 1))\f$ | — | — | 18, 615 [27.0] | 2.8, 1557 [5.0] | — |
| \f$\mathrm{sech}^p\f$ | — | — | 6.3, 182 [10.0] | 3.7, 1561 [6.0] | — |

Integrands like \f$x^{a-1}\f$ require nodes below \f$10^{-16/a}\f$ of scale, so \f$a < 0.06\f$ exceeds binary64 range. Fixed Gauss rules are exact for their polynomial weights on smooth integrands. See `docs/numerics.md` for rejected tolerances, oscillatory cancellation, and defect repairs.

## Speed

Throughput over 69,120 integrals (540 rows repeated 128 times) in \f$\mu\mathrm{s}\f$ per integral on gcc (1 thread), OpenMP (16 threads), and CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER); cost is dominated by 90 to 1500 evaluations.

| Rule | gcc, 1 thread (\f$\mu\mathrm{s}\f$) | OpenMP, 16 threads (\f$\mu\mathrm{s}\f$) | CUDA, RTX 4070 SUPER (\f$\mu\mathrm{s}\f$) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `tanh_sinh_quad()` | 17.3 | 1.51 | 3.44 |
| `exp_sinh_quad()` | 10.0 | 0.91 | 0.82 |
| `sinh_sinh_quad()` | 20.8 | 1.79 | 2.62 |
| `gauss_kronrod_quad()` | 61 | 4.4 | 3.9 |
| `panels_quad()` | 121 | 9.5 | 8.7 |
| Fixed Gauss rules | 2.1–2.7 | 0.26–0.32 | 0.20 |

## Example

Evaluating definite integrals inside `Kokkos::parallel_for`:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/math/quadrature.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void compute_integrals(Kokkos::View<double*>& values,
                       Kokkos::View<int*>& evals,
                       int n) {
  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_quadrature", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    auto integrand = [](double x) {
      return Kokkos::exp(-x * x);
    };
    const auto res = laplace::math::quad::tanh_sinh_quad(integrand, -1.0, 1.0);
    values(i) = res.value;
    evals(i)  = res.evals;
  });
}
\endcode

## Module types and functions

All symbols below belong to `laplace::math::quad`.

### Types

- \ref laplace::math::quad::quad_result "quad_result" — Adaptive result (`value`, `error`, `evals`, `converged`).
- \ref laplace::math::quad::de_quad_options "de_quad_options" — Double-exponential options (`stop`, `max_level`).

### Adaptive rules

- \ref laplace::math::quad::tanh_sinh_quad "tanh_sinh_quad()" — Tanh-sinh on \f$[a, b]\f$.
- \ref laplace::math::quad::exp_sinh_quad "exp_sinh_quad()" — Exp-sinh on \f$[a, \infty)\f$.
- \ref laplace::math::quad::sinh_sinh_quad "sinh_sinh_quad()" — Sinh-sinh on \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$.
- \ref laplace::math::quad::gauss_kronrod_quad "gauss_kronrod_quad()" — Adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7–15 on \f$[a, b]\f$.
- \ref laplace::math::quad::panels_quad "panels_quad()" — Geometric panels at centre and kinks.

### Fixed rules

- \ref laplace::math::quad::gauss_legendre_quad "gauss_legendre_quad()" — Fixed 16/32/64-node Gauss-Legendre on \f$[a, b]\f$.
- \ref laplace::math::quad::gauss_hermite32_quad "gauss_hermite32_quad()" — 32-node Gauss-Hermite on \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$.
- \ref laplace::math::quad::gauss_laguerre32_quad "gauss_laguerre32_quad()" — 32-node Gauss-Laguerre on \f$[a, \infty)\f$.

## References

- H. Takahasi and M. Mori (1974), Double exponential formulas for numerical integration, Publications of the Research Institute for Mathematical Sciences, 9(3), 721–741.
*/